---
import { BP4_SUPPORTING, PP3_SUPPORTING } from '../../src/constants.ts'
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { oddsText } from '../lib/charts.ts'
import { gpcr, summary } from '../lib/data.ts'
import { percent } from '../lib/html.ts'
import { href } from '../lib/site.ts'

const s = summary()
const data = gpcr()
const chimeras = data.complexes.filter(c => c.gProteins.some(g => g.chimera)).length
const t = s.thresholds
const uncertain = s.alphaMissense.calls.uncertain
const HAN = 'https://research.nvidia.com/labs/dbr/assets/data/manuscripts/afdb.html'

const references = [
  ['Han, Tsenkov, Venanzi et al. 2026. AlphaFold Database expands to proteome-scale quaternary structures. Preprint.', HAN],
  ['Evans et al. 2021. Protein complex prediction with AlphaFold-Multimer. bioRxiv.', 'https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.10.04.463034'],
  ["Dunbrack 2025. Rēs ipSAE loquuntur: what's wrong with AlphaFold's ipTM score and how to fix it. bioRxiv.", 'https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.10.637595'],
  ['Varadi et al. 2024. AlphaFold Protein Structure Database in 2024. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkad1011'],
  ['Szklarczyk et al. 2023. The STRING database in 2023. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkac1000'],
  ['The UniProt Consortium 2025. UniProt: the Universal Protein Knowledgebase in 2025. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkae1010'],
  ['Landrum et al. 2018. ClinVar: improving access to variant interpretations and supporting evidence. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkx1153'],
  ['Cheng et al. 2023. Accurate proteome-wide missense variant effect prediction with AlphaMissense. Science.', 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg7492'],
  ['Bergquist et al. 2025. Calibration of additional computational tools expands ClinGen recommendation options for variant classification with PP3/BP4 criteria. Genet Med.', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gim.2025.101402'],
  ['Richards et al. 2015. Standards and guidelines for the interpretation of sequence variants. Genet Med.', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/gim.2015.30'],
  ['Nightingale et al. 2017. The Proteins API: accessing key integrated protein and genome information. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkx237'],
  ['Pollard et al. 2010. Detection of nonneutral substitution rates on mammalian phylogenies. Genome Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.097857.109'],
  ['Dana et al. 2019. SIFTS: updated Structure Integration with Function, Taxonomy and Sequences resource. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gky1114'],
  ['Pándy-Szekeres et al. 2023. GPCRdb in 2023. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkac1013'],
  ['Mantel & Haenszel 1959. Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease. J Natl Cancer Inst.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/jnci/22.4.719'],
  ['Sehnal et al. 2021. Mol* Viewer: modern web app for 3D visualization and analysis of large biomolecular structures. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkab314'],
  ['Diesh et al. 2023. JBrowse 2: a modular genome browser with views of synteny and structural variation. Genome Biol.', 'https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-023-02914-z'],
] as const
---

<Layout title="About" nav="about">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An explorer for asking which partner a disease variant might disturb: predicted protein–protein interfaces and the ClinVar variants on them, in 3D and on the genome.
  </p>
  <h2>Method</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All {s.proteins.toLocaleString()} reviewed human proteins with a UniProt disease annotation.</li>
    <li>
      Their heterodimers from <a href={HAN}>AlphaFold DB's 2026 complex release</a>, predicted with
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.10.04.463034">AlphaFold-Multimer</a> for STRING physical-interaction pairs.
      AlphaFold DB publishes only those it calls tentatively high-confidence: <a
        href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.10.637595">ipSAE</a
      > ≥ {t.ipsae} in both directions (the lower of chain A → B and B → A), mean pLDDT ≥ 70 and at most 10 backbone clashes. Where AlphaFold DB modelled the partner with another species' protein, the partner is
      named as the human protein of identical sequence; where a partner was modelled twice, the more confident complex is kept.
    </li>
    <li>
      Doubtful pairs: {s.pairs.apart} predicted pairs join a protein UniProt places only inside mitochondria (outer membrane
      aside) with one it never places in mitochondria, such as the cytosolic and mitochondrial aspartyl-tRNA synthetases DARS1
      and DARS2; they are marked "different compartments". {s.pairs.paralogs.toLocaleString()} pair a protein with a member of
      its own UniProt family. Paralogs do form real complexes, such as α- and β-tubulin, but AlphaFold can also model one
      protein's homodimer interface across two family members, so the {s.pairs.paralogsWithoutStructure.toLocaleString()} with
      no PDB entry holding both are marked "paralogs, no structure of both". Neither mark removes a pair from the counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Interface residues: any heavy atom within {t.contact} Å of the partner, residue pLDDT ≥ {t.plddt}. Mapped to hg38
      codons with EBI Proteins API coordinates.
    </li>
    <li>
      Variants: single-nucleotide missense variants classified pathogenic, likely pathogenic, uncertain, likely benign or
      benign in NCBI's weekly ClinVar <code>variant_summary</code> (downloaded {s.provenance.clinvarDownloaded}), on a RefSeq
      transcript UniProt maps to the canonical isoform. Conflicting classifications are left out, and so is any variant whose
      wild-type residue does not match the canonical sequence at its position.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each variant's <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg7492">AlphaMissense</a> pathogenicity, from the published
      table of every substitution in the canonical proteins. Of the {s.variantsAtInterface.uncertain.toLocaleString()}
      variants of uncertain significance at an interface, it scores {uncertain.scored.toLocaleString()}. Read with the ClinGen
      calibration of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gim.2025.101402">Bergquist et al. 2025</a>, it gives
      {uncertain.pp3.toLocaleString()} ({percent(uncertain.pp3, uncertain.scored)}) at least supporting evidence of
      pathogenicity (ACMG PP3, score ≥ {PP3_SUPPORTING}) and {uncertain.bp4.toLocaleString()}
      ({percent(uncertain.bp4, uncertain.scored)}) evidence of benignity (BP4, ≤ {BP4_SUPPORTING}). The developers'
      0.564 "likely pathogenic" cut-off does not reach supporting evidence, so the site does not use it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Conservation: the phyloP score of each coding base in UCSC's 100-way vertebrate alignment to hg38 (multiz100way), read
      from UCSC's bigWig. A residue's conservation is the mean over its codon.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Does the interface mean anything?</h2>
  <p>Within each protein, pathogenic missense variants sit at a predicted interface more often than benign ones:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>All residues: <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.all)} /></li>
    <li>
      Only folded residues (pLDDT ≥ 70): <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.ordered)} />. The buried core is rich in
      pathogenic variants and is not an interface.
    </li>
    <li>Only folded, surface-exposed residues: <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.orderedSurface)} /></li>
    <li>
      The same, comparing variants only within the same quarter of vertebrate conservation:
      <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.conservation.enrichment.orderedSurface.matched)} />
    </li>
    {
      Object.entries(s.enrichment.byAlphaMissenseClass).map(([name, e]) => (
        <li>
          Only variants in the class "{name}": <Fragment set:html={oddsText(e)} />
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    Mantel–Haenszel odds ratios across proteins, with intervals from resampling proteins. The surface line rules out "folded
    vs disordered" as the explanation, the AlphaMissense lines show the interface adds little to a predictor that never sees
    the partner, and only among variants it already gives pathogenic evidence, and the conservation line that conservation explains only part of it. Each confidence band of
    ipSAE, compared with residues at no interface, gives about the same odds ratio. The
    <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-disease-variants-cluster/')}>findings post</a> draws these as charts, and
    <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-are-the-interfaces-real/')}>another</a> checks the predicted interfaces against
    experimental structures and against random patches of the same protein's surface.
  </p>
  <h2>Leads</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={href('leads/')}>{s.leads.variants.toLocaleString()} leads</a> are the variants of uncertain significance at
    a confident interface residue where ClinVar classifies another missense variant pathogenic. Conditions are the names
    ClinVar's submitters gave, so one disease can appear under several names.
  </p>
  <h2>GPCRs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      {data.complexes.filter(c => c.dataset === 'DataSet1').length} experimental receptor–G protein complexes of biogenic
      amine receptors, from a GPCRdb selection made by BioHackathon Europe 2026 Project 9, plus {data.others.length} receptor
      structures without a G protein. One olfactory receptor complex, OR51E2 with mini-Gs (8F76), is added to test AlphaFold
      DB's predicted Gαolf interfaces; <code>gpcr_structures.tsv</code> marks it as added. The
      <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-project-9-gpcrs/')}>GPCR post</a> shows what they find.
    </li>
    <li>
      Contacts: heavy atoms within 5 Å between receptor and Gα. PDBe's SIFTS annotation gives each residue's UniProt
      position; a receptor chain SIFTS does not map is aligned to its UniProt sequence. Non-human proteins are aligned to the
      human ortholog. Receptor residues are labelled with GPCRdb generic numbers from GPCRdb's residue table for the human
      receptor, or their segment (e.g. ICL3) where it gives none.
    </li>
    <li>
      {chimeras} G proteins are engineered chimeras that SIFTS maps to no gene. Each stretch of such a chain is assigned to
      the human Gα it matches best, favouring the Gα the structure is annotated with.
    </li>
    <li>Every receptor and Gα here is looked up in AlphaFold DB, whether or not it is a disease protein.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Isoforms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      AlphaFold DB's monomer models of UniProt's alternative isoforms, for disease genes with at least one modelled besides
      the canonical.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each isoform's sequence is rebuilt from UniProt's alternative-sequence edits and checked against its model's sequence,
      which gives every isoform residue its canonical position. A replacement of the same length is compared residue by
      residue, so only residues that change count as lost.
    </li>
    <li>RefSeq transcripts come from UniProt's cross-references, which name the isoform each one encodes.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Coverage: {percent(s.withPartner, s.proteins)} of disease proteins have any predicted partner. AlphaFold DB modelled
      STRING physical-interaction pairs only; TP53 has none and KRAS has no RAF.
    </li>
    <li>
      The complexes are predictions. AlphaFold DB calls ipSAE 0.6–0.7 low confidence, 0.7–0.8 confident and ≥ 0.8 very
      high confidence, and the enrichment is about the same in each band.
    </li>
    <li>
      The enrichment itself is not new; the <a href={href('comparison/')}>comparison</a> lists who showed it first and what
      this explorer adds.
    </li>
    <li>An interface variant is a lead, not a mechanism: the residue may matter for folding or for another partner.</li>
    <li>
      Experimental interfaces from the PDB are shown in the
      <a href="https://genomes.jbrowse.org/protein-browser/">protein browser</a>; this explorer adds predicted ones.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>References</h2>
  <ul class="references">
    {
      references.map(([text, url]) => (
        <li>
          {text} <a href={url}>{url.replace('https://', '')}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</Layout>
